\begin{document}

\title{Maxwell--Jones Vector-Sensor Localization and Synchronization for
Distributed Near-Field RIS-Aided OFDM}

\author{Qingshan Gong,~\IEEEmembership{Member,~IEEE}}

\markboth{IEEE Transactions on Wireless Communications,~Vol.~XX, No.~XX, 2026}%
{Gong: Maxwell--Jones Vector-Sensor Localization and Synchronization}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
This paper studies joint 3-D localization and clock synchronization of a
single-antenna user seen by one electromagnetic vector-sensor (EVS) array
only through distributed near-field reconfigurable intelligent surfaces
(RISs), in a wideband orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (OFDM)
uplink with unknown gains, Jones coefficients and panel labels. A per-panel
space--frequency/Maxwell--Jones factorization carries three structural
consequences. Component selection rescales a panel's local information but
leaves panel discrimination to a separate subspace condition, so a reduced
receiver can lose separability outright while every local bound stays finite.
The calibrated Maxwell factors confine every admissible
response to a receive subspace that does not depend on the unknowns, so a
sufficient compression ($96\to6$ here) is available before acquisition and
preserves the profiled maximizer and the free-Jones information exactly. The
common clock acts on the Jones Gram matrix as an invariance, reducing
the conditional clock dependence to a finite trigonometric polynomial whose
global maximizer carries a deterministic optimality gap and whose elimination
leaves a 3-D search. The resulting two-phase estimator gains in acquisition
reliability rather than in local precision: at $-10$~dB SNR it attains
$0.32$-mm median position error with $0.42\%$ and $0.31\%$ position and clock
catastrophic rates, against $2.50\%$ and $2.08\%$ for the closest in-model
four-dimensional control at matched median accuracy.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Clock synchronization, EVS, near-field
localization, OFDM,
RIS, variable projection.
\end{IEEEkeywords}


\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\IEEEPARstart{R}{econfigurable} intelligent surfaces (RISs) make the
propagation environment itself programmable, and are studied as a low-cost
way to shape coverage in future wireless systems~\cite{wymeersch2020risloc}.
As their apertures grow electrically large at millimeter-wave and
sub-terahertz frequencies, the users they serve fall inside the radiative
near field, where the plane-wave approximation no longer holds and the
curvature of the wavefront across the aperture carries range in addition to
direction~\cite{cui2023nearfield}. That extra geometric
content turns an electrically large surface from a communication device into
a passive anchor, able to fix a user in three dimensions without active
infrastructure of its own~\cite{elzanaty2021bounds}. Deploying several such
panels adds spatially diverse reflected paths, which matters most when the
direct link to the base station (BS) is obstructed. A passive RIS provides
no independent timing reference, however, so the delays measured through such
a deployment carry
the clock offset of the user equipment (UE) as well as its position, and the
two have to be recovered together. This joint problem lies at the
intersection of three largely separate lines of work: RIS-aided
localization and synchronization, near-field and
extremely-large-aperture estimation, and polarimetric sensing.

RIS-aided localization and synchronization has moved from single-panel
geometry to distributed deployments~\cite{ma2023distributed,liu2025multiris}
and to formulations carrying an unknown clock
explicitly~\cite{fascista2022rissync,gong2024async,peng2025sdp,yan2025ris,fadakar2024multiris}.
Near-field and extremely-large-aperture estimation supplies both the
spherical-wave models this regime needs and the acquisition front ends that
make them tractable: multilinear factorization of the training
tensor~\cite{lin2021tensor}, sparse and variational recovery on a
discretized manifold~\cite{li2024vbi}, and polar or beam-split codebooks
matched to wavefront
curvature~\cite{cui2022channel,yang2024nfris,zheng2025wideband}. In
polarimetric sensing, polarization enters RIS work mainly as a controllable
resource -- dual-polarized surfaces spend it on beamforming
gain~\cite{han2022dual} -- whereas array processing treats it as an unknown:
an electromagnetic vector sensor (EVS) measures co-located electric and
magnetic fields and so resolves the polarization state of an incident wave
alongside its direction~\cite{nehorai1994vector,khan2020sixcomponent}. Our
own EVS-OFDM (orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing)
estimator~\cite{gong2026aimdf} applies such an array to a direct multipath
channel, without the distributed geometry, panel labeling, compression or
shared clock at issue here.

