Despite these advances, the resulting estimation problem remains unresolved
because the limitations of the three lines compound in this architecture.
First, distributed RIS localization and synchronization typically resolve
panel identities before or during the joint
fit~\cite{ma2023distributed,liu2025multiris,fascista2022rissync}. Here, all
cascades reach the same EVS array within one observation and an algebraic
front end returns them unlabeled~\cite{lin2021tensor}; two panels at nearly
coincident cascaded delays present increasingly similar space--frequency
evidence. Their separability then also depends on the retained Maxwell
structure of the receiver, whereas existing polarimetric processing mainly
treats polarization as an additional estimation
dimension~\cite{nehorai1994vector,khan2020sixcomponent}. Second, acquisition
becomes most demanding in the large-aperture wideband regime that motivates
near-field processing: full-EVS training produces six field components over
$M_A$ sensors, $N$ subcarriers and $T$ RIS configurations, while an arbitrary
linear reduction provides no guarantee of preserving the delay subspace,
profiled likelihood or localization information. Fixed discretizations used by
sparse and codebook-based front ends~\cite{li2024vbi,cui2022channel} also
become increasingly coarse relative to the aperture resolution, while
restoring that resolution requires a denser dictionary and a larger search
burden. Third, the shared clock is either discarded or retained as an extra
nonlinear variable. Differencing cascaded OFDM delays removes the common
offset but leaves only $K-1$ differences for three position coordinates, which
is underdetermined at $K=3$, whereas retaining the absolute delays leaves a
nonconvex four-dimensional position--clock problem. Variable projection
eliminates the linear nuisances but not this coupling~\cite{golub1973varpro},
while the semidefinite route to a certified global solution~\cite{peng2025sdp}
addresses timing models that lift to a linear one, not the OFDM likelihood
here. Table~\ref{tab:comparison} places the closest formulations against the
capabilities this architecture needs: none carries more than three of the
six, and the architecture considered here exposes all three limitations at
once.

We address these limitations through one structural property of the
cascaded observation, not through three separate devices. The sensing block of
the $k$th RIS panel factorizes as
\begin{equation}
	\bA_k(\bchi)=
	\underbrace{\bc_k(\bchi)}_{\text{space--frequency geometry}}
	\otimes
	\underbrace{\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}}_{\text{calibrated Maxwell--Jones}},
	\label{eq:premise}
\end{equation}
where $\bchi=[\bp^{\mathsf T},\Delta t]^{\mathsf T}$ collects the unknown
position and common clock, $\bc_k$ carries all of that dependence, and
$\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}$ -- two columns for the polarization state, one row
per retained field component -- is fixed by the calibrated RIS-to-BS geometry
and the receiver selection. The unknown geometry and clock are therefore
separated from the calibrated polarimetric response. Three consequences
follow.

\textbf{C1. In a polarimetric array, local information and source
discrimination are separate resources.} A Cram\'er--Rao analysis does not
tell them apart. The Kronecker form splits the orthogonal projector into a
geometric and a polarimetric term, so component selection rescales a panel's
free-Jones information by the polarization energy it retains and cannot
rotate the information eigenvectors. Discrimination obeys a different
condition. Two panels sharing a space--frequency signature are separated only
by a residual in the retained Maxwell subspace, and that residual vanishes
outright once two or fewer components are kept and the competing retained
Jones block has full row rank -- while every local bound stays finite. This
is what makes a full EVS receiver worth its six chains, and a bound map
cannot show it (Theorem~\ref{thm:split};
Figs.~\ref{fig:c1_receiver} and~\ref{fig:c1_separability}).

\textbf{C2. Calibration makes sufficiency a property of the infrastructure,
not of the estimate.} A sufficient statistic is normally formed after a
parameter is known well enough to define it. Here the calibrated Maxwell
factors confine every admissible response to a receive subspace of dimension
at most $2K$ that does not depend on $\bchi$, so the projection is fixed at
deployment and applied before any parameter is estimated. It preserves the
profiled likelihood maximizer, the parameter derivatives of the residual
criterion to every order, and the free-Jones information exactly. In the
reference scene it takes $96$ receive channels to $6$, and it is that
reduction, not a change of criterion, that carries the catastrophic position
rate from $2.92\%$ to none of $480$ trials at $-10$~dB
(Theorem~\ref{thm:compress}; Table~\ref{tab:c2_ladder}).

\textbf{C3. A nuisance that acts as a common unitary phase ramp can be
maximized globally and removed from the outer search.} Such a nuisance leaves
the profiled Gram matrix invariant, so after the linear coefficients are
eliminated its conditional profile is a finite trigonometric polynomial -- a
structure that admits a certified global maximizer at a deterministic
optimality gap, and whose elimination costs the outer search one dimension.
The shared clock is one instance. One factorization per visited position
supplies every clock coefficient by a single fast Fourier transform (FFT);
the inner search never refactorizes; the outer problem drops from four
dimensions to three and becomes invariant to the clock unit, which an
explicit four-dimensional coordinate is not -- expressing that coordinate in
seconds costs $32\%$ in conditional position RMSE
(Theorem~\ref{thm:clock}; Table~\ref{tab:c3_control}).

Gaussian sufficiency for a known signal subspace, and the elimination of
linear parameters by variable projection~\cite{golub1973varpro}, are
classical; what \eqref{eq:premise} adds is that calibration fixes such a
subspace without knowing $\bchi$. The delay front end
reuses the asymmetric tensor folding of~\cite{gong2026aimdf} unchanged; the
sufficient compression preceding it and the common-geometry fusion following
it are not part of that work.

C2 and C3 are what the implementation runs on. C2 defines the compressed
representation on which Phase~I builds a physically associated near-field
initializer, and on which Phase~II then evaluates the exact forward model
without approximation. C3 supplies Phase~II's refinement. There the Jones
coefficients are eliminated analytically and the certified clock profile
removes the clock from the outer search, leaving a three-dimensional position
refinement regularized toward the Phase-I Jones anchor. C1 is a condition on
the receiver, not an algorithmic ingredient, and it is the result that
generalizes furthest: the same splitting applies to any array whose response
factorizes into a calibrated polarimetric block and a parameter-dependent
geometric one.

Notation: bold symbols denote vectors/matrices; $(\cdot)^{\mathsf T}$,
$(\cdot)^{\mathsf H}$ and $(\cdot)^{\dagger}$ are transpose, Hermitian
transpose and pseudoinverse; $\otimes$ and $\odot$ are Kronecker and
Hadamard products; for a generic matrix $\bX$, $\bP_{\bX}=\bX\bX^{\dagger}$
and $\bPi_{\bX}^{\perp}=\bI-\bP_{\bX}$ are the orthogonal projectors onto
$\coll(\bX)$ and $\coll(\bX)^{\perp}$; and unqualified vector and matrix
norms are Euclidean and spectral, respectively.

\section{Maxwell--Jones Cascaded Signal Model}
\label{sec:system_model}

We consider a wideband uplink in which, with the line-of-sight (LoS) path
blocked, a single-antenna UE reaches the BS only through $K$ distributed,
arbitrarily oriented RIS panels~\cite{fascista2022rissync,zhao2021timing};
each panel contributes one dominant cascaded component, so the model order is
the known panel count $K$. Calibration fixes the BS phase center and every
panel position and rotation, leaving the UE position $\bp$ and clock offset
$\Delta t$ unknown. Each panel is an $M_{Rx}\times M_{Ry}$ planar array of
$M_R=M_{Rx}M_{Ry}$ elements and the BS an $M_A$-element EVS uniform linear
array (ULA), and the UE transmits pilots on $N$ subcarriers spaced by
$\Delta f$ (occupied bandwidth $B_{\rm occ}=(N-1)\Delta f$) over $T$ known RIS
phase configurations within one coherence block. Following the one-sided
near-field convention of~\cite{yang2024nfris,cui2022channel}, the UE--RIS hop
carries an exact spherical-wave phase over a uniform-amplitude aperture and
the RIS--BS hop a center-frequency plane wave, verified for the reference
scene of Section~\ref{sec:simulation}.

The received signal is
\begin{equation}
	\by=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\Big[
	\underbrace{\bh_k(\bp)}_{\text{near field}}\otimes
	\underbrace{\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)}_{\text{delay}}\otimes
	\underbrace{\bBm_k}_{\text{calibrated}}\Big]\bx_k+\bn,
	\label{eq:roadmap}
\end{equation}
with $\by,\bn\in\C^{M}$, $M=I\,N\,T$ for the receive dimension $I$ of
Section~\ref{subsec:calibrated}, and $\bx_k\in\C^2$ a free Jones coefficient;
only $\bh_k$ and $\bd_k$ depend on the unknown $(\bp,\Delta t)$. The three
factors are derived next.

\begin{figure*}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{EVS_RIS_system_model-crop.pdf}
\caption{Cascaded geometry and parameterization: (a)~global frame,
    (b)~local frame of panel $k$, and (c)~six-component EVS element with the
    receiver selections.}
\label{fig:geometry}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Calibrated RIS--BS Maxwell Factor}
\label{subsec:calibrated}

Calibration provides $\bp_B$, the BS phase center; $\br_k$ and
$\bR_k\in{\rm SO}(3)$, the center and the global-to-local rotation of panel
$k$; $\brho_{k,m}\in\R^3$, its element coordinates in that frame; and the BS
array geometry and EVS Maxwell response. The BS ULA lies along the global $x$ axis of the
orthonormal frame $\{\bm u_x,\bm u_y,\bm u_z\}$ and
$\kappa=2\pi/\lambda_c$ is the center-frequency wavenumber.

The calibrated locations give the RIS--BS distance and propagation direction
of panel $k$,
\begin{equation}
	d_{{\rm RB},k}=\|\bp_B-\br_k\|_2,
	\qquad
	\bs_{{\rm RB},k}=\frac{\bp_B-\br_k}{d_{{\rm RB},k}}
	\label{eq:rbgeom}
\end{equation}
and, under the far-field RIS--BS model established above, a plane-wave panel
response and a BS steering vector,
\begin{align}
	\big[\ba_{{\rm RB},k}\big]_m
	&=e^{j\kappa\,\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bR_k\bs_{{\rm RB},k}},\nonumber\\
	\big[\bv_{{\rm B},k}\big]_i
	&=e^{-j\kappa x_i\,\bm u_x^{\mathsf T}\bs_{{\rm RB},k}},
	\label{eq:arb_vb}
\end{align}
with $x_i$ the $x$ coordinate of the $i$th BS element,
$\ba_{{\rm RB},k}\in\C^{M_R}$ and $\bv_{{\rm B},k}\in\C^{M_A}$.

The same direction fixes the polarization manifold: a transverse plane wave
along $\bs_{{\rm RB},k}$ has Maxwell pair
$(\bE,\bH)=(\bq,\bs_{{\rm RB},k}\times\bq)$ in impedance-normalized units, so
with any transverse basis
\begin{equation}
	\bq_{k,1}\in\bs_{{\rm RB},k}^{\perp},
	\quad \|\bq_{k,1}\|_2=1,
	\qquad
	\bq_{k,2}=\bs_{{\rm RB},k}\times\bq_{k,1},
	\label{eq:qbasis}
\end{equation}
the six-component
vector-sensor manifold~\cite{nehorai1994vector,khan2020sixcomponent}
specializes to the calibrated Maxwell--Jones block
\begin{equation}
	\bTheta_k^{(6)}=\begin{bmatrix}
	\bq_{k,1} & \bq_{k,2}\\[1pt]
	\bq_{k,2} & -\bq_{k,1}\end{bmatrix}\in\C^{6\times2},
	\label{eq:Theta}
\end{equation}
whose columns satisfy
$[\bTheta_k^{(6)}]^{\mathsf H}\bTheta_k^{(6)}=2\bI_2$; a rotation
$\bU\in{\rm SO}(2)$ of the transverse basis sends
$\bTheta_k^{(6)}\mapsto\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bU$ and is absorbed by
$\bx_k\mapsto\bU^{\mathsf T}\bx_k$. Deployment
calibration is assumed to equalize per-component gains and cross-channel
coupling~\cite{bayraktar2024risloc}, so \eqref{eq:Theta} is the
post-calibration EVS response. A fixed
receiver selection $\bS_{\rm mode}\in\R^{d_{\rm evs}\times6}$,
\begin{equation}
	\bS_1=\begin{bmatrix}1 & \mathbf 0\end{bmatrix},
	\quad
	\bS_2=\begin{bmatrix}\bI_2 & \mathbf 0\end{bmatrix},
	\quad
	\bS_6=\bI_6,
	\label{eq:smode}
\end{equation}
with $d_{\rm evs}\in\{1,2,6\}$ for the scalar, dual-polarized and full-EVS
receivers of Fig.~\ref{fig:geometry}(c), gives
$\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}\triangleq\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}
\in\C^{d_{\rm evs}\times2}$ and the receive dimension $I=d_{\rm evs}M_A$.

The calibrated receive block
$\bBm_k\triangleq\bv_{{\rm B},k}\otimes\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}\in\C^{I\times2}$
is therefore known before UE estimation and satisfies
$\partial\bBm_k/\partial\bchi=\mathbf0$; only the Jones-domain coefficients
$\bx_k\in\C^2$ of \eqref{eq:roadmap} remain unknown, each being the effective
polarization state launched toward the BS after reflection rather than the UE
antenna state.

\subsection{UE--RIS Near-Field and Common-Clock Factor}
\label{subsec:defchain}

The unknown nonlinear parameter collects the UE position and the single
clock, $\bchi=[\bp^{\mathsf T},\Delta t]^{\mathsf T}
=[x,y,z,\Delta t]^{\mathsf T}\in\R^4$, with $\bp\in\mathcal P$ a known closed
service box and $\Delta t\in\mathcal T$ a known closed interval,
$|\mathcal T|<1/\Delta f$ the unambiguous delay period. Every cascaded delay
is a geometric path length plus this one
offset~\cite{fascista2022rissync,gong2024async,yan2025ris};
panel-dependent offsets~\cite{zhao2021timing} lie outside the model.
In the $k$th panel frame (Fig.~\ref{fig:geometry}(b)),
$\bxi_k(\bp)=\bR_k(\bp-\br_k)$ and
\begin{equation}
	d_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)=\|\bxi_k(\bp)\|_2=\|\bp-\br_k\|_2,
	\quad
	\bs_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)=\frac{\bxi_k(\bp)}{d_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)},
	\label{eq:urgeom}
\end{equation}
since $\bR_k$ is a rotation. Element $m$ of panel $k$
applies the scalar Jones response $e^{j\omega_{k,t,m}}\bI_2$ at configuration
$t$, so no polarization mixing occurs; with the prescribed constant-modulus
training $[\bOmega_k]_{t,m}=e^{j\omega_{k,t,m}}/\sqrt{M_R}$,
$\bOmega_k\in\C^{T\times M_R}$~\cite{lin2021tensor}, the near-field
dependence is captured by the RIS response
\begin{align}
	\bg_k(\bp) &= \ba_{{\rm RB},k}\odot\ba^{\rm NF}_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)
	\in\C^{M_R},\nonumber\\
	\bh_k(\bp) &= \bOmega_k\,\bg_k(\bp)\in\C^{T},
	\label{eq:hk}
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
	\big[\ba^{\rm NF}_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)\big]_m
	&= e^{-j\frac{2\pi}{\lambda_c}\delta_{k,m}(\bp)},
	\quad \ba^{\rm NF}_{{\rm UR},k}\in\C^{M_R},\nonumber\\
	\delta_{k,m}(\bp)
	&= \big\|\bxi_k(\bp)-\brho_{k,m}\big\|_2
	- d_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp).
	\label{eq:delta}
\end{align}
Equation~\eqref{eq:delta} retains the exact spherical-wave phase across the
panel aperture (Fig.~\ref{fig:geometry}(b)); the Fresnel approximation enters
only in Phase-I proposal generation (Section~\ref{subsec:beamspace}).

Combining \eqref{eq:rbgeom} and \eqref{eq:urgeom}, the cascaded delay and its
OFDM phase ramp on the tone grid
$f_n=f_c+\big(n-\tfrac{N-1}{2}\big)\Delta f$, $n=0,\ldots,N-1$, are
\begin{align}
	\tau_k(\bp,\Delta t)
	&= \underbrace{\frac{d_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp) + d_{{\rm RB},k}}{c_0}}_{
	\textstyle \tau_{g,k}(\bp)} + \Delta t,\nonumber\\
	\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)
	&= \Big[\,e^{-j2\pi\left(n-\frac{N-1}{2}\right)\Delta f\,
	\tau_k(\bp,\Delta t)}\,\Big]_{n=0}^{N-1}\in\C^N,
	\label{eq:dk}
\end{align}
with $c_0$ the speed of light and the tone-independent phase
$e^{-j2\pi f_c\tau_k}$ absorbed into the path gain. The per-subcarrier form of \eqref{eq:dk}
assumes the cyclic prefix covers the cascaded delay spread and neglects
residual carrier-frequency offset and phase noise. Unlike the
frequency-dependent spatial manifold of~\cite{yang2024nfris}, spatial
manifolds are evaluated here at the carrier $f_c$ and the wideband dependence
is carried by the delay phase on every subcarrier. With $\Delta L$ an
inter-element path difference of a spatial response, the neglected
spatial-wideband phase
$\epsilon_{\rm sq}=\pi B_{\rm occ}\max|\Delta L|/c_0$ peaks at $0.102$~rad
over the three spatial responses of the reference configuration (design
condition and per-panel audit in the supplement).

The bracketed factor of \eqref{eq:roadmap} is the per-panel sensing block,
which by associativity of the Kronecker product also takes the
form~\eqref{eq:premise},
\begin{align}
	\bA_k(\bchi)
	&= \bh_k(\bp)\otimes\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)\otimes\bBm_k\nonumber\\
	&= \bc_k(\bchi)\otimes\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}\in\C^{M\times2},
	\label{eq:Ak}
\end{align}
where $\bc_k(\bchi)=\bh_k(\bp)\otimes\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)\otimes
\bv_{{\rm B},k}\in\C^{TNM_A}$ is the space--frequency factor. Entry
$(t-1)NI+nI+i$ of $\by$ carries receive channel $i=1,\ldots,I$ and subcarrier
$n=0,\ldots,N-1$ at configuration $t=1,\ldots,T$; the scalar sample expansion
and pilot normalization are in the supplement.

Equation~\eqref{eq:Ak} admits two useful groupings. The first,
$\bh_k\otimes\bd_k\otimes\bBm_k$, exposes the calibrated receive subspace:
since
$\bA_k\bx_k=\bh_k\otimes\bd_k\otimes(\bBm_k\bx_k)$, reading the $I$ receive
channels against the $N$ subcarriers at configuration $t$ arranges the same
samples as
\begin{equation}
	\bY_t=\sum_{k=1}^{K}[\bh_k(\bp)]_t\,(\bBm_k\bx_k)\,
	\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)^{\mathsf T}+\bN_t,
	\label{eq:obsmat}
\end{equation}
$t=1,\ldots,T$, with $\bY_t\in\C^{I\times N}$ and $\bN_t$ the noise block. Each panel is therefore rank
one across receive and frequency at fixed $t$, its receive factor confined to
the $\bchi$-independent $\coll(\bBm_k)$ of dimension at most two. The second
grouping,
$\bc_k\otimes\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}$, confines all dependence on
$\bS_{\rm mode}$ to $\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}$ and all position--clock
dependence to the mode-independent $\bc_k(\bchi)$, with $\Delta t$ entering
only through $\bd_k$. Section~\ref{sec:algorithm} works in the first
arrangement and Section~\ref{sec:analysis} in the second.

\subsection{Observation and Estimation Problem}
\label{subsec:obsmodel}

Stacking the panels as
$\bA(\bchi)=[\bA_1(\bchi),\ldots,\bA_K(\bchi)]\in\C^{M\times 2K}$ and
vectorizing the $\bY_t$ in the same order writes \eqref{eq:roadmap} as
\begin{equation}
	\by = \bmu(\bchi,\bx) + \bn = \bA(\bchi)\,\bx + \bn,
	\qquad
	\bn\sim\mathcal{CN}(\mathbf 0,\sigma^2\bI_M),
	\label{eq:obs}
\end{equation}
where the noise is white across receive channels, subcarriers and RIS
configurations, with power $\sigma^2$ \emph{not} provided to the estimator. The unknown Jones-domain coefficient of path $k$
is $\bx_k=\beta_k\be_k\in\C^2$, stacked as
$\bx=[\bx_1^{\mathsf T},\ldots,\bx_K^{\mathsf T}]^{\mathsf T}\in\C^{2K}$,
where the complex gain $\beta_k$ absorbs path loss, RIS efficiency and the
center-frequency phase of the cascade and $\be_k$ is a unit Jones state
(angular parameterization in the supplement). Both factors are flat over the
occupied band and only their product is identifiable, so the estimator
treats $\bx_k$ as a free $\C^2$ nuisance.

Profiling $\sigma^2$ out of the white-Gaussian log-likelihood gives
$\widehat\sigma^2=\|\by-\bA(\bchi)\bx\|_2^2/M$ and a concentrated criterion
strictly decreasing in the residual energy, so maximum-likelihood (ML)
estimation of $(\bchi,\bx)$ is the least-squares problem
\begin{equation}
	\min_{\bp\in\mathcal P,\ \Delta t\in\mathcal T,\ \bx\in\C^{2K}}
	\big\|\by-\bA(\bp,\Delta t)\,\bx\big\|_2^2.
	\label{eq:ml_problem}
\end{equation}
The $2K$ Jones coefficients enter linearly and admit closed-form
elimination, whereas position and clock enter nonlinearly through
\eqref{eq:delta} and \eqref{eq:dk}, leaving \eqref{eq:ml_problem} nonconvex
in four real coordinates.

\section{Proposed Estimator: Sufficient Acquisition and Certified Clock Profiling}
\label{sec:algorithm}

Direct optimization of \eqref{eq:ml_problem} couples the panel
association, the near-field basin, the $2K$ linear Jones coefficients and
the common clock. We separate them with a two-phase estimator. Phase~I,
\emph{Maxwell--Kronecker sufficient compression with common-geometry
initialization} (MKSC-GI), recovers and associates the cascaded factors
inside a calibration-fixed sufficient subspace and fuses the panel-local
estimates into one common position initializer. Phase~II,
\emph{common-clock orbit-profiled Jones variable projection} (CCOP-JVP),
eliminates the Jones coefficients analytically and refines position and
clock by certified profiling along the exact clock orbit.
Algorithm~\ref{alg:mksc_ccop} collects both phases; the equation number on
each step anchors it to the derivation below.

\begin{algorithm}[!t]
\footnotesize
\caption{MKSC-GI Initialized CCOP-JVP}
\label{alg:mksc_ccop}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE $\{\bY_t\}_{t=1}^{T}$, calibrated infrastructure, $K$,
$\mathcal P$, $\mathcal T$
\STATE \textbf{Phase~I: MKSC-GI}
\STATE Compress receive index: $\bY_{{\rm d},t}=\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}\bY_t$,
$I\!\to\!r\le2K$ \hfill\eqref{eq:calibsub}
\STATE Delay poles $\{\hat z_k\}$ by ESPRIT on the tone axis
\hfill\eqref{eq:shift}
\STATE Coupled factors $\hat\bF$ by one loaded WLS solve
\hfill\eqref{eq:coupledls_short}
\STATE Rank-one split $\to(\tilde\ba_j,\tilde\bt_j,\varrho_j)$
\hfill\eqref{eq:split_rank1}
\STATE Panel labels $\hat\pi$ by two-tier permutation search
\hfill\eqref{eq:assoc_perm}
\FOR{$k=1,\ldots,K$}
\STATE Dechirped 2-D DFT proposals, re-scored by $J_{\rm RIS}$
\hfill\eqref{eq:Jris_short}
\ENDFOR
\STATE Fuse by $J_{\rm geom}$; select by clock spread
\hfill\eqref{eq:Jgeom_short}
\STATE Jones anchors $\{\hat\be_k\}$ refreshed at $\hat\bp_{\rm GI}$
\hfill\eqref{eq:refresh_short}
\STATE \textbf{Phase~II: CCOP-JVP}
\REPEAT
\STATE Clock-profile coefficients $\{\gamma_\ell(\bp)\}$ by one FFT
\hfill\eqref{eq:trig_short}
\STATE Branch and bound on $\Delta t$:
$g_{\rm cert}\!\le\!\epsilon_{\rm clk}$ or cap \hfill\eqref{eq:bound_short}
\STATE Update $\bp$ by envelope gradient, safeguard otherwise
\hfill\eqref{eq:envelope}
\UNTIL{convergence}
\STATE Profiled Jones coefficients $\hat\bx=\bG_\lambda^{+}\bb$
\hfill\eqref{eq:vp_short}
\RETURN $(\hat\bp,\widehat{\Delta t},\hat\bx)$ and $g_{\rm cert}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Maxwell--Kronecker Factor Recovery}

\textit{1) Sufficient compression and delay-pole recovery:}
The calibrated receive blocks in \eqref{eq:Ak} span
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal S_R=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\coll(\bBm_k)=\coll(\bPsi),
 \quad \bPsi=[\bBm_1,\ldots,\bBm_K],
 \label{eq:calibsub}
\end{equation}
with $r=\rank\bPsi\le2K$. An orthonormal basis $\bQ_S\in\C^{I\times r}$
depends only on calibration and satisfies
$\bQ_S\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}\bBm_k=\bBm_k$ for every panel. Thus
\begin{equation}
 \bY_{{\rm d},t}=\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}\bY_t
 =\sum_{k=1}^{K}[\bh_k(\bp)]_t\,\ba_k\,
 \bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)^{\mathsf T}+\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}\bN_t,
 \label{eq:obsmat_c}
\end{equation}
where $\ba_k=\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}\bBm_k\bx_k$.
Theorem~\ref{thm:compress} establishes sufficiency: the reference scene
reduces from $I=96$ channels to $r\le6$, discarding noise-only directions
while retaining white noise in the compressed coordinates.

Each delay contributes the pole $z_k=e^{-j2\pi\Delta f\tau_k}$.
The centered tone grid adds only a nonzero column scalar:
$[\bd_k]_n=e^{j\pi(N-1)\Delta f\tau_k}z_k^n$.
Rearranging the compressed channel--configuration pairs as snapshots gives
\begin{equation}
 \bY_f=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\bd_k\,\bw_k^{\mathsf T}+\bN_f\in\C^{N\times rT},
 \qquad \bw_k=\ba_k\otimes\bh_k(\bp).
 \label{eq:freqsnap}
\end{equation}
For $\bD=[\bd_1,\ldots,\bd_K]$, removing its last or first row gives
$\underline{\bD}$ or $\overline{\bD}$, respectively, with
\begin{equation}
 \overline{\bD}=\underline{\bD}\,\boldsymbol\Phi,
 \qquad \boldsymbol\Phi=\diag(z_1,\ldots,z_K).
 \label{eq:shift}
\end{equation}
Distinct delays modulo $1/\Delta f$, $N\ge K+1$ and the snapshot-rank
condition of Theorem~\ref{thm:compress}(iv) therefore permit recovery by
forward--backward total-least-squares ESPRIT
\cite{roy1989esprit,gong2026aimdf}, with estimated poles projected onto the
unit circle.

\textit{2) Coupled factor recovery:}
With the estimated delays fixed, recover
$\bF=[\bw_1,\ldots,\bw_K]\in\C^{rT\times K}$ from the rearranged data
$\bY_{rT,N}$ by
\begin{equation}
 \hat\bF=\arg\min_{\bF}
 \big\|(\bY_{rT,N}-\bF\bD^{\mathsf T})\bW^{1/2}\big\|_F^2
 +\lambda_\tau\|\bF\|_F^2,
 \label{eq:coupledls_obj}
\end{equation}
or, equivalently, one $K\times K$ solve:
\begin{equation}
 \hat\bF=\bY_{rT,N}\bW\bD^{\ast}
 (\bD^{\mathsf T}\bW\bD^{\ast}+\lambda_\tau\bI)^{-1}.
 \label{eq:coupledls_short}
\end{equation}
Here $\bW=\diag(w_n)$, with $w_n=\#\{(p,\ell):p+\ell=n\}$ for the
$P\times L$ smoothing window, $P+L-1=N$; the loading is fixed in the supplement.
Reshape column $j$ as an $r\times T$ matrix with receive index running
slowest, so $\unvec(\ba\otimes\bt)=\ba\bt^{\mathsf T}$.
Its leading singular triplet gives
\begin{equation}
 \tilde\ba_j=\sqrt{\sigma_{1,j}}\,\bQ_S\bu_{1,j},\quad
 \tilde\bt_j=\sqrt{\sigma_{1,j}}\,\bv_{1,j}^{\ast},\quad
 \varrho_j=\frac{\sigma_{2,j}}{\sigma_{1,j}}.
 \label{eq:split_rank1}
\end{equation}
For the generating panel $k$, the noiseless factors satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \tilde\ba_j=c_j\,\bBm_k\bx_k,\qquad
 \tilde\bt_j=c_j'\,\bh_k(\bp),
 \label{eq:factor_meaning}
\end{equation}
where $c_j,c_j'\ne0$ encode the scale and phase ambiguity.
Hence subsequent criteria must be scale invariant; $\varrho_j$ measures
departure from rank one and weights the common-geometry fit.

\textit{3) Physical panel association:}
For column $j$ and candidate panel $k$, combine calibrated receive-subspace
and spherical-wave consistency:
\begin{equation}
 s_{j,k}=
 \frac{\|\bPi^{\perp}_{\bBm_k}\tilde\ba_j\|}{\|\tilde\ba_j\|}
 +\min_{\boldsymbol\eta\in\mathcal E_k}
 \frac{\|\bPi^{\perp}_{\bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)}\tilde\bt_j\|}
 {\|\tilde\bt_j\|}.
 \label{eq:assoc_score}
\end{equation}
Here $\boldsymbol\eta_k=(d_{{\rm UR},k},u_k,v_k)$ contains range and the
first two direction components, $\mathcal E_k$ is the panel-local search
box, and $\bh_k$ is the exact response \eqref{eq:hk}.
The minimizing range $\hat d_{j,k}$ implies a clock replica
$\widehat{\Delta t}_{j,k}=\hat\tau_j-(\hat d_{j,k}+d_{{\rm RB},k})/c_0$.
Score a permutation by
\begin{equation}
 C(\pi)=\sum_{j=1}^{K}s_{j,\pi(j)}
 +\frac{w_{\rm clk}}{t_0}
 [\,\mathrm{sd}(\pi)+e_{\mathcal T}(\pi)\,],
 \label{eq:assoc_perm}
\end{equation}
where $e_{\mathcal T}$ is the mean excursion outside $\mathcal T$,
$w_{\rm clk}=1/2$, $t_0=1$~ns, and
\begin{align}
 \mathrm{sd}(\pi)&=\Big[\tfrac1K\sum_{j=1}^{K}
 (\widehat{\Delta t}_{j,\pi(j)}-\overline{\Delta t}(\pi))^2
 \Big]^{1/2},\nonumber\\
 \overline{\Delta t}(\pi)&=\tfrac1K\sum_{j=1}^{K}
 \widehat{\Delta t}_{j,\pi(j)}.
 \label{eq:assoc_sd}
\end{align}
The shared center couples the assignments, so this is not a linear
assignment problem. A deterministic two-tier search ranks all $K!$
permutations using a cached coarse codebook, then re-scores only the best
two, $\pi_{(1)},\pi_{(2)}$, using exact spherical projection:
\begin{equation}
 \hat\pi=\arg\min_{\pi\in\{\pi_{(1)},\pi_{(2)}\}}C(\pi),\qquad
 m_{\rm assoc}=\frac{C_{(2)}-C_{(1)}}{C_{(1)}+\epsilon}.
 \label{eq:assoc_two_tier}
\end{equation}
The re-scored costs satisfy $C_{(1)}\le C_{(2)}$; the supplement reports the
margin $m_{\rm assoc}$. It returns the labeled delay--factor triples
$\{\hat\tau_j,\tilde\ba_j,\tilde\bt_j;\hat\pi(j)\}$ for geometric acquisition.

\subsection{Beam-Space Acquisition and Common-Geometry Initialization}
\label{subsec:beamspace}

\textit{1) Panel-local beam-space acquisition:}
By \eqref{eq:factor_meaning} the factor $\tilde\bt_k$ that $\hat\pi$ assigns
to panel $k$ is $\bh_k$ up to an unknown scale, so the geometry it carries is
read back out one panel at a time, before the $K$ readings are reconciled
into a single position, by the scale-invariant fit
\begin{equation}
 J_{\rm RIS}(\boldsymbol\eta)
 =\|\bPi_{\bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)}^\perp\tilde\bt_k\|^2
 =\|\tilde\bt_k\|^2-
 \frac{|\bh_k^{\mathsf H}(\boldsymbol\eta)\tilde\bt_k|^2}
 {\|\bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)\|^2},
 \label{eq:Jris_short}
\end{equation}
with $\bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)=\bOmega_k[\ba_{{\rm RB},k}\odot
\ba^{\rm NF}(\boldsymbol\eta)]$ over the panel-local coordinates
$\boldsymbol\eta$ defined above. A fixed-count angular dictionary of the polar
or beam-training kind~\cite{cui2022channel,zheng2025wideband} has a step set
by the search box, not by the panel resolution $\lambda_c/D$, so the dominant
angular search is moved to the element domain. The training code enters
$\bh_k$ only on the left, so moving $\bOmega_k^{\mathsf H}$ across and
splitting the Hadamard product gives
\begin{align}
 \bh_k^{\mathsf H}(\boldsymbol\eta)\tilde\bt_k
 &=\big[\ba_{{\rm RB},k}\odot\ba^{\rm NF}(\boldsymbol\eta)\big]^{\mathsf H}
 \big(\bOmega_k^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bt_k\big)
 =\big[\ba^{\rm NF}(\boldsymbol\eta)\big]^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bg_k,\nonumber\\
 \tilde\bg_k&=\ba_{{\rm RB},k}^{\ast}\odot
 (\bOmega_k^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bt_k).
 \label{eq:adjoint_short}
\end{align}
One adjoint pass fixes $\tilde\bg_k$ per panel and leaves
$\ba^{\rm NF}(\boldsymbol\eta)$ as the only $\boldsymbol\eta$-dependent factor
in the numerator of \eqref{eq:Jris_short}; the denominator admits no such
reduction and is evaluated exactly. Expanding \eqref{eq:delta} to second
order about the panel center,
\begin{equation}
	\delta_{k,m}\simeq-\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bu
	+\frac{\|\brho_{k,m}\|^2-(\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bu)^2}{2\tilde d},
	\label{eq:fresnel}
\end{equation}
so the paraxial dechirp $\tilde\bg_{k,\tilde d}[m]=\tilde\bg_k[m]
e^{j\kappa\|\brho_{k,m}\|^2/(2\tilde d)}$ removes the isotropic quadratic term
at trial range $\tilde d$ and leaves the plane-wave phase
$e^{j\kappa\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bu}$, up to the direction-dependent
curvature $(\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bu)^2/(2\tilde d)$. One zero-padded 2-D
discrete Fourier transform (DFT) then scores every direction cosine at once on
a grid tracking the Rayleigh limit, at one transform per trial range and no
dictionary scanned atom by atom. That residual curvature makes
\eqref{eq:fresnel} a proposal only: every visible angular peak receives an
exact spherical range scan and is re-scored by the full
\eqref{eq:Jris_short}, so the candidate set changes acquisition coverage and
cost but not the forward model. Each experiment's set is stated in
Section~\ref{sec:simulation}.

\textit{2) Common-geometry fusion:}
Each panel-local fix $\hat{\boldsymbol\eta}_k$ maps through the calibrated
panel pose back to a global position
$\hat\bp_k^{\rm loc}=\br_k+\bR_k^{\mathsf T}\bxi_k(\hat{\boldsymbol\eta}_k)$,
inverting $\bxi_k(\bp)=\bR_k(\bp-\br_k)$. These $K$ positions generally
disagree; the common-geometry stage replaces them by the single position that
best explains all $K$ training factors at once, each up to its own unknown
complex scale,
\begin{align}
 J_{\rm geom}(\bp)
 &=\min_{\balpha\in\C^K}\sum_{k=1}^K \varpi_k
 \|\tilde\bt_k-\alpha_k\bh_k(\bp)\|^2
 \label{eq:Jgeom_short}\\
 &=\sum_{k=1}^K \varpi_k\left[\|\tilde\bt_k\|^2-
 \frac{|\bh_k^{\mathsf H}(\bp)\tilde\bt_k|^2}{\|\bh_k(\bp)\|^2}\right],
 \nonumber
\end{align}
the second line following from the profiled scale
$\hat\alpha_k(\bp)=\bh_k^{\mathsf H}(\bp)\tilde\bt_k/\|\bh_k(\bp)\|^2$, so
only the component of $\tilde\bt_k$ orthogonal to $\coll(\bh_k)$ survives and
no gain need be known. The weights
$\varpi_k=\max\{e^{-\varrho_k/\varrho_0},\varpi_{\min}\}$ down-weight a column
whose rank-one split is poor while keeping every panel in the fit. We minimize
\eqref{eq:Jgeom_short} with bounded L-BFGS-B~\cite{byrd1995lbfgsb} from
deterministic starts around
$\hat\bp_0=\clip_{\mathcal P}\{\median_k\hat\bp_k^{\rm loc}\}$, and select
$\hat\bp_{\rm GI}$ as the near-optimal minimum whose clock replicas
$\hat\tau_k-(\|\bp-\br_k\|+d_{{\rm RB},k})/c_0$ vary least; constants are
supplementary.

\textit{3) Jones-anchor refresh:}
At the selected $\hat\bp_{\rm GI}$, the $\lambda_k=0$ instance of the
certified clock profiler of Section~\ref{subsec:ccop} refreshes the Jones
directions at a fixed position,
\begin{equation}
 (\hat{\Delta t}_{\rm ref},\hat\bx_{\rm ref})=
 \arg\min_{\Delta t\in\mathcal T,\bx}
 \|\by-\bA(\hat\bp_{\rm GI},\Delta t)\bx\|^2.
 \label{eq:refresh_short}
\end{equation}
With $\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}$ the $k$th two-entry block of $\hat\bx_{\rm ref}$,
the anchor is $\hat\be_k=\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}/\|\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}\|_2$,
obtained at the common position from a conditional clock solve that carries
the same optimality-gap certificate. Phase~I therefore outputs
$\hat\bp_{\rm GI}$ and the anchors $\{\hat\be_k\}$, which initialize the
Phase-II directional regularizer.

\subsection{Common-Clock Orbit-Profiled Jones Variable Projection}
\label{subsec:ccop}

\textit{1) Unitary clock orbit and directional Jones regularization:}
Phase~II returns from the basin just built to the exact forward model
underlying \eqref{eq:ml_problem}, augmented by a directional Jones penalty.
Its $2K$ Jones coefficients enter linearly and are eliminated in
closed form by variable projection~\cite{golub1973varpro}, leaving four
nonlinear coordinates. By Theorem~\ref{thm:compress} the criterion may be
evaluated on the compressed observation of Phase~I without approximation,
which is what the implementation does. The common clock is the only nonlinear
coordinate along which the likelihood admits an exact unitary symmetry. Let
$\bA_0(\bp)\triangleq\bA(\bp,0)$ and
$\bD_{\rm clk}(\Delta t)=\diag([e^{-j2\pi(n-\frac{N-1}{2})\Delta f\Delta t}]_{n=0}^{N-1})$.
Then
\begin{align}
 \bU_{\rm clk}(\Delta t)&=\bI_T\otimes\bD_{\rm clk}(\Delta t)\otimes\bI_I,
 \nonumber\\
 \bA(\bp,\Delta t)&=\bU_{\rm clk}(\Delta t)\bA_0(\bp),
 \label{eq:orbit_short}
\end{align}
and unitarity makes the Jones Gram clock invariant,
$\bA^{\mathsf H}\bA=\bA_0^{\mathsf H}\bA_0\triangleq\bG_0(\bp)$.
Phase~II uses a Gram-scaled block-diagonal penalty
\begin{equation}
 \bR_{\rm dir}(\bp)=\blkdiag_k\left[
 \lambda_k\tfrac12\tr\bG_{0,kk}(\bp)\,\bPi_{\hat\be_k}^{\perp}\right],
 \label{eq:Rdir_short}
\end{equation}
which penalizes only the component of $\bx_k$ orthogonal to the refreshed
anchor $\hat\be_k$; in particular $\bR_{{\rm dir},kk}\hat\be_k=\mathbf0$, so
the anchor direction is left free while the gain $\beta_k$ remains an
unconstrained nuisance, which a penalty on
$\|\bx_k-\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}\|$ would not achieve. What the penalty must
repair is set entirely by the receiver selection. Each Kronecker factor of
\eqref{eq:Ak} contributes its own Gram, and $\bd_k$ and
$\bv_{{\rm B},k}$ have unit-modulus entries, so
\begin{equation}
	\bG_{0,kk}=N M_A\|\bh_k(\bp)\|^2\,
	[\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}]^{\mathsf H}\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}.
	\label{eq:g0block}
\end{equation}
The receiver therefore fixes how many Jones coordinates the data can see,
\begin{equation}
	\rank\bG_{0,kk}=\rank\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}
	=\min\{d_{\rm evs},2\},
	\label{eq:g0rank}
\end{equation}
generically in the panel orientations. Full-EVS reception gives
$[\bTheta_k^{(6)}]^{\mathsf H}\bTheta_k^{(6)}=2\bI_2$ by \eqref{eq:Theta} and
$\bG_{0,kk}\succ\mathbf0$; a scalar mask leaves $\bG_{0,kk}$ singular, with one
Jones coordinate per panel invisible. Supported on the complement of
$\hat\be_k$, $\bR_{\rm dir}$ restores that coordinate at the Phase-I anchor
without displacing the identifiable one. Blockwise definiteness is not by
itself the hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:clock}: for positive semidefinite
$\bG_0$ and $\bR_{\rm dir}$,
\begin{equation}
	\bG_\lambda\succ\mathbf0
	\iff
	\ker(\bG_0)\cap\ker(\bR_{\rm dir})=\{\mathbf0\},
	\label{eq:glambda_pd}
\end{equation}
so panels whose space--frequency signatures are linearly dependent -- the
degeneracy of Theorem~\ref{thm:split}(ii) -- can leave $\bG_\lambda$
singular with every diagonal block invertible. No ridge is added to repair
this. The implementation tests the rank of $\bG_\lambda$ and, when
\eqref{eq:glambda_pd} fails, evaluates $Q$ with the Moore--Penrose inverse
$\bG_\lambda^{+}$, which minimizes $\|\bx\|$ among the maximizers of the
same criterion rather than changing it; because $\bG_\lambda$ is
clock-independent, the substitution leaves the clock profile a trigonometric
polynomial of the same degree. The
dimensionless weight is $\lambda_k=1$ throughout, so the penalty enters at
the scale of the per-path data Gram (Remark~\ref{rem:peb_reading}); how far
it is from active at the returned solution is measured in
Section~\ref{sec:simulation}.

\textit{2) Jones variable projection and the trigonometric clock profile:}
Phase~II accordingly minimizes the penalized exact-forward-model criterion
\begin{equation}
 J(\bp,\Delta t,\bx)=\|\by-\bA(\bp,\Delta t)\bx\|_2^2
 +\bx^{\mathsf H}\bR_{\rm dir}(\bp)\bx,
 \label{eq:penalized_short}
\end{equation}
whose first term is the nominal criterion \eqref{eq:ml_problem}. It is
\eqref{eq:penalized_short}, not \eqref{eq:ml_problem}, that
Theorem~\ref{thm:clock} nests and certifies; the two coincide at
$\lambda_k=0$, and the supplement reports the $\lambda$ sweep.
Substituting \eqref{eq:orbit_short} into \eqref{eq:penalized_short} and using
$\bU_{\rm clk}^{\mathsf H}\bU_{\rm clk}=\bI$ makes $J$ a quadratic in $\bx$
whose Hessian carries no clock dependence,
\begin{equation}
 J=\|\by\|_2^2-2\Real\{\bx^{\mathsf H}\bb(\bp,\Delta t)\}
 +\bx^{\mathsf H}\bG_\lambda(\bp)\bx,
 \label{eq:jquad}
\end{equation}
with $\bG_\lambda=\bG_0+\bR_{\rm dir}$, which reduces to $\bG_0$ at
$\lambda_k=0$, and $\bb=\bA_0^{\mathsf H}\bU_{\rm clk}^{\mathsf H}\by$. The
Wirtinger stationarity condition
$\partial J/\partial\bx^{\ast}=\bG_\lambda\bx-\bb=\mathbf0$ hence removes all
$2K$ Jones coefficients in one step,
\begin{align}
 \hat\bx&=\bG_\lambda^{-1}\bb,
 \quad Q(\bp,\Delta t)=\bb^{\mathsf H}\bG_\lambda^{-1}\bb,\nonumber\\
 F(\bp,\Delta t)&\triangleq\min_{\bx\in\C^{2K}}J=\|\by\|_2^2-Q,
 \label{eq:vp_short}
\end{align}
leaving the 3-D outer objective
\begin{equation}
 \widetilde F(\bp)=\|\by\|^2-
 \max_{\Delta t\in\mathcal T}Q(\bp,\Delta t).
 \label{eq:nested_short}
\end{equation}
Grouping the sufficient-statistic correlations by tone as
$\bb(\bp,\Delta t)=\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\bu_n(\bp)
e^{j2\pi(n-\frac{N-1}{2})\Delta f\Delta t}$ and carrying them through the
clock-independent $\bG_\lambda^{-1}$,
\begin{align}
 Q(\bp,\Delta t)
 &=\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\sum_{n'=0}^{N-1}
 \bu_n^{\mathsf H}\bG_\lambda^{-1}\bu_{n'}\,
 e^{j2\pi(n'-n)\Delta f\Delta t}\nonumber\\
 &=\sum_{\ell=-(N-1)}^{N-1}\gamma_\ell(\bp)
 e^{j2\pi\ell\Delta f\Delta t},\quad \gamma_{-\ell}=\gamma_\ell^\ast,
 \label{eq:trig_short}
\end{align}
with $\gamma_\ell(\bp)=\sum_{n'-n=\ell}\bu_n^{\mathsf H}(\bp)
\bG_\lambda^{-1}(\bp)\bu_{n'}(\bp)$: the clock survives only through tone
differences, so at fixed $\bp$ the profile $Q$ is a real trigonometric
polynomial of degree at most $N-1$, and one factorization of $\bG_\lambda$
with one zero-padded FFT supplies all initial clock candidates. That
factorization is formed once per visited position and serves the entire inner
clock search: the certified maximization below evaluates
\eqref{eq:trig_short} and its derivative on intervals and never refactorizes.
Differentiating \eqref{eq:trig_short} twice gives
$Q''=-(2\pi\Delta f)^2\sum_\ell\ell^2\gamma_\ell
e^{j2\pi\ell\Delta f\Delta t}$, so the curvature is bounded uniformly on
$\mathcal T$ by $L_2\triangleq(2\pi\Delta f)^2\sum_\ell\ell^2|\gamma_\ell|$
and second-order Taylor expansion about the center $t_c$ of an interval of
half-width $r_t$ yields the branch bound
\begin{equation}
 Q(t)\le Q(t_c)+|Q'(t_c)|r_t+\tfrac12L_2r_t^2.
 \label{eq:bound_short}
\end{equation}
\textit{3) Certified clock maximization and outer refinement:}
Branch-and-bound maintains \eqref{eq:bound_short} over $\mathcal T$ and
returns an incumbent with
$Q(\Delta t^\star)-Q(\hat{\Delta t})\le U-Q(\hat{\Delta t})
\triangleq g_{\rm cert}$, where $U=\max\{U_{\rm act},U_{\rm pru}\}$ is the
largest bound over surviving and incumbent-pruned intervals; subdivision stops once $g_{\rm cert}\le\epsilon_{\rm clk}$ or a cap
on subdivisions is reached. Every outer position therefore carries a
deterministic gap certificate, $\epsilon_{\rm clk}$-optimal whenever
termination is by tolerance, as Theorem~\ref{thm:clock}(iii) states. The outer bounded search starts from $\hat\bp_{\rm GI}$ and, where the
maximizing branch is unique and interior, uses the envelope-theorem gradient
\begin{equation}
	\nabla_{\bp}\widetilde F(\bp)
	=-\nabla_{\bp}Q(\bp,\Delta t)\big|_{\Delta t=\hat{\Delta t}(\bp)},
	\label{eq:envelope}
\end{equation}
with a derivative-free safeguard~\cite{powell1964direct} otherwise. Phase~II outputs
$(\hat\bp,\widehat{\Delta t},\hat\bx)$ together with the certificate attached
to every conditional clock solve it evaluated. Tolerances and the safeguard
trigger are supplementary.


\section{Structural Analysis and Guarantees}
\label{sec:analysis}

This section formalizes C1--C3 under the conditions stated with each result.
After the free-Jones reference information and its local rank condition, the
three guarantees follow in turn from the Kronecker projector structure of
\eqref{eq:premise}, from its calibrated receive subspace, and from its
common-clock unitary orbit.

\textit{A. Reference information and identifiability:}
Let $\bD_\chi=[\partial(\bA\bx)/\partial\chi_i]_{i=1}^4$. Eliminating the
unknown complex Jones coefficients from the white-Gaussian Fisher
information matrix gives the free-Jones equivalent Fisher information
matrix (EFIM), the reference information of the unpenalized data model,
\begin{equation}
 \bJ_\chi^{\rm free}=\frac{2}{\sigma^2}
 \Real\{\bD_\chi^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}.
 \label{eq:efim_short}
\end{equation}
Local position--clock identifiability after eliminating the Jones nuisance
requires
\begin{equation}
 \rank_{\R}[\Real\{\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}^{\mathsf T},
 \Imag\{\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}^{\mathsf T}]^{\mathsf T}=4,
 \label{eq:rankcond}
\end{equation}
equivalently $\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}\succ\mathbf0$, that matrix being the Gram
of the real stacking. It then yields the position and clock error bounds
(PEB, CEB) ${\rm PEB}=\sqrt{\tr[(\bJ_p^{\rm eff})^{-1}]}$ and
${\rm CEB}=\sqrt{[(\bJ_\chi^{\rm free})^{-1}]_{4,4}}$, with
$\bJ_p^{\rm eff}=\bJ_{pp}-\bJ_{pt}\bJ_{tt}^{-1}\bJ_{tp}$. The delay-only
Jacobian of \eqref{eq:dk} is
\begin{equation}
	\bD_\tau(\bp)
	=\frac{\partial[\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_K]^{\mathsf T}}
	{\partial\bchi^{\mathsf T}}
	=\begin{bmatrix}
	c_0^{-1}\bs_{{\rm UR},1}^{\mathsf T}\bR_1 & 1\\
	\vdots & \vdots\\
	c_0^{-1}\bs_{{\rm UR},K}^{\mathsf T}\bR_K & 1
	\end{bmatrix}\in\R^{K\times4},
	\label{eq:delayjac}
\end{equation}
whose all-ones clock column is what row differencing removes. At $K=3$,
$\rank\bD_\tau\le3<4$, so the cascaded delays cannot identify $\bchi$ by
themselves; the near-field aperture supplies the missing direction, because
after complex-scale elimination the projected tangents
$\bPi_{\bh_k}^{\perp}[\partial\bh_k/\partial x,\partial\bh_k/\partial y,
\partial\bh_k/\partial z]$ retain the phase slope and curvature of
\eqref{eq:delta}. The test is local and conditions on the panel assignment, with pseudoinverse
projectors covering the rank-deficient Jones blocks of \eqref{eq:g0rank}. The
derivation of \eqref{eq:efim_short} and of the real-rank equivalence is
supplementary.

\textit{B. C1---Maxwell--Jones information splitting:}
Both parts of C1 rest on one identity: because $\bA_k$ is a Kronecker
product, so is its projector, and the complement separates into a
polarization-orthogonal component along the geometric factor and a
geometry-orthogonal component acting identically on every polarization.

\begin{lemma}[Kronecker projector splitting]
\label{lem:kron}
Let $\bc\neq\mathbf0$ and $\bTheta\neq\mathbf0$ be arbitrary, with
$\bP_{\bTheta}=\bTheta\bTheta^{\dagger}$. With $\bA=\bc\otimes\bTheta$,
\begin{equation}
 \bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}
 =\bP_{\bc}\otimes\bPi_{\bTheta}^{\perp}+\bPi_{\bc}^{\perp}\otimes\bI,
 \qquad \bP_{\bc}=\frac{\bc\bc^{\mathsf H}}{\|\bc\|^2}.
 \label{eq:kronsplit}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{IEEEproof}
$\bA^{\mathsf H}\bA=(\bc^{\mathsf H}\bc)(\bTheta^{\mathsf H}\bTheta)$ and
$\bA^{\dagger}=\bc^{\dagger}\otimes\bTheta^{\dagger}$, so
$\bP_{\bA}=\bP_{\bc}\otimes\bP_{\bTheta}$. Expanding
$\bI=(\bP_{\bc}+\bPi_{\bc}^{\perp})\otimes(\bP_{\bTheta}
+\bPi_{\bTheta}^{\perp})$ and subtracting $\bP_{\bA}$ gives
\eqref{eq:kronsplit}. No rank assumption is used, so the identity survives
the component mask of C1(ii), which can make the retained Jones block rank
deficient.
\end{IEEEproof}

\begin{theorem}[C1: Maxwell--Jones information splitting]
\label{thm:split}
\textbf{(i) Information consequence.} For one panel, write
$\bA=\bc\otimes\bTheta$, $\boldsymbol{\zeta}=\bTheta\bx$,
$\dot\bc_i=\partial\bc/\partial\chi_i$, and
$\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i=(\partial\bTheta/\partial\chi_i)\bx$. Then
\begin{equation}
 [\bD_\chi^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi]_{ij}
 =\|\boldsymbol{\zeta}\|^2\dot\bc_i^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bc}^\perp\dot\bc_j
 +\|\bc\|^2\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i^{\mathsf H}
 \bPi_{\bTheta}^\perp\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_j.
 \label{eq:split_short}
\end{equation}
Since calibrated infrastructure gives
$\partial\bTheta/\partial\bchi=\mathbf0$, the second term vanishes.
Throughout C1, $\bTheta=\bTheta_k^{(6)}$ is the \emph{full} block of
\eqref{eq:Theta} and $\bS_{\rm mode}$ one of the selections
\eqref{eq:smode}, so for a single panel with nonzero retained signal
\begin{equation}
	\bJ_\chi^{\bS_{\rm mode}}=\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}\bJ_\chi^{\rm full},
	\quad
	{\rm PEB}^{\bS_{\rm mode}}
	=\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}^{-1/2}\,{\rm PEB}^{\rm full},
	\label{eq:c1_scaling}
\end{equation}
with $\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}
=\|\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta\bx\|^2/\|\bTheta\bx\|^2$, the ${\rm PEB}$ identity
requiring in addition $\bJ_\chi^{\rm full}\succ\mathbf0$ so that both bounds
are finite.

\textbf{(ii) Discrimination consequence.} Define
$\bA_k^{(\bS)}(\bchi)=\bc_k(\bchi)\otimes
\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}$. If a competitor block matches panel $k$'s
space--frequency signature, i.e.
$\bc_{k'}(\bchi')\parallel\bc_k(\bchi)$, its free-Jones residual is
\begin{equation}
 \|\bPi_{\bA_{k'}^{(\bS)}(\bchi')}^\perp
 \bA_k^{(\bS)}(\bchi)\bx_k\|
 =\|\bc_k\|\,\|\bPi_{\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}}^\perp
 \bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k\|.
 \label{eq:panelres_short}
\end{equation}
If the retained competitor block
$\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}\in\C^{d_{\rm evs}\times2}$ has full row rank
for $d_{\rm evs}\le2$, this residual is zero. With all six components it is at least
$\|\bc_k\|\|\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k\|\sin\theta_{\min}$, where
$\theta_{\min}>0$ is the minimum principal angle between the two panel
Maxwell subspaces, positive whenever they are distinct.
\end{theorem}

\begin{IEEEproof}
(i) A Jacobian column of $\bA\bx=\bc\otimes\boldsymbol{\zeta}$ is
$\bD_i=\dot\bc_i\otimes\boldsymbol{\zeta}
+\bc\otimes\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i$. Insert Lemma~\ref{lem:kron} into
$\bD_i^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_j$ and apply the mixed-product
rule: every cross term carries a factor $\bPi_{\bc}^{\perp}\bc=\mathbf0$ or
$\bPi_{\bTheta}^{\perp}\boldsymbol{\zeta}=\mathbf0$, leaving the two terms
of \eqref{eq:split_short}. A mask replaces $\bTheta$ by
$\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta$ and leaves $\bc$ untouched, so with
$\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i=\mathbf0$ the whole matrix scales by
$\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}$.

(ii) Apply \eqref{eq:kronsplit} to the competitor
$\bA_{k'}^{(\bS)}(\bchi')$ and evaluate it at
$\bc_k\otimes\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k$. Under
$\bc_{k'}\parallel\bc_k$ the term $\bPi_{\bc_{k'}}^{\perp}\otimes\bI$
annihilates its argument, leaving
$\bP_{\bc_{k'}}\otimes\bPi^{\perp}_{\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}}$ and
hence \eqref{eq:panelres_short}. If
$\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}\in\C^{d_{\rm evs}\times2}$ has row rank
$d_{\rm evs}\le2$ its range is all of $\C^{d_{\rm evs}}$ and that projector is
zero. For $d_{\rm evs}=6$ the two Maxwell subspaces are proper subspaces of
$\C^6$ separated by a minimum principal angle $\theta_{\min}>0$, so
$\|\bPi_{\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}}^{\perp}\bz\|\ge\sin\theta_{\min}\|\bz\|$ for
every $\bz\in\coll(\bTheta_k^{(6)})$, which is the stated bound.
\end{IEEEproof}

C1 says that polarization enters the geometry as a scalar: by
\eqref{eq:c1_scaling} component selection cannot rotate a panel's
information eigenvectors, only rescale them by the polarization energy it
retains. The scaling is per panel:
\begin{equation}
	\bJ_\chi^{\bS_{\rm mode}}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\alpha_k\,
	\bJ_{\chi,k}^{\rm full},
	\qquad
	\alpha_k=\frac{\|\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k\|^2}
	{\|\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k\|^2},
	\label{eq:c1_aggregate}
\end{equation}
which holds when the panel blocks and their projected tangents are mutually
orthogonal. Away from that case the $\alpha_k$ enter unevenly and no single
scalar rescales the aggregate. Part (ii) is
a statement of a different kind: a wrong-panel hypothesis distance rather
than a local perturbation, which vanishes for $d_{\rm evs}\le2$ under its
full-row-rank condition while the information of (i) merely rescales. Local
information and cross-panel discrimination are therefore separate
consequences of receiver choice, and only the first is visible to a local
bound.

\begin{remark}[Scope of the free-Jones reference bound]
\label{rem:peb_reading}
The free-Jones PEB is the bound of the unpenalized data model. Because
$\lambda_k=1$ carries no taper in $\sigma$, the penalized estimator's
departure from it is measured rather than bounded: over
$\lambda_k\in\{0,0.1,1,10\}$ the conditional position RMSE moves by less than
$2\times10^{-5}$ in relative terms (supplementary).
\end{remark}

\textit{C. C2---Sufficient Maxwell--Kronecker compression:}
\begin{theorem}[C2: Sufficient Maxwell--Kronecker compression]
\label{thm:compress}
Let $\bU_S=\bI_{NT}\otimes\bQ_S$ be the semi-unitary lifting matrix in the
vectorization of \eqref{eq:obs}, and suppose
$\coll[\bA(\bchi)]\subseteq\coll(\bU_S)$ for all admissible $\bchi$. Write
$\by_c=\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\by$ and
$c_\perp=\|(\bI-\bU_S\bU_S^{\mathsf H})\by\|^2$. Under the matched
white-Gaussian model:
(i) for known $\sigma^2$, $\by_c$ is sufficient for $(\bchi,\bx)$;
(ii) for unknown $\sigma^2$, the pair $(\by_c,c_\perp)$ is sufficient for
$(\bchi,\bx,\sigma^2)$, and $\by_c$ alone still preserves the maximizer in
$(\bchi,\bx)$ of the $\sigma^2$-profiled likelihood, because
\begin{equation}
 \|\by-\bA(\bchi)\bx\|^2
 =\|\by_c-\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\bA(\bchi)\bx\|^2+c_\perp
 \label{eq:isometry_short}
\end{equation}
and $c_\perp$ does not depend on $(\bchi,\bx)$;
(iii) $\by_c$ alone preserves the profiled optimizer, the derivatives of
$\|\by-\bA(\bchi)\bx\|^2$ in $(\bchi,\bx)$ to every order, and the free-Jones
EFIM \eqref{eq:efim_short} exactly;
(iv) the noiseless delay poles are preserved provided the compressed
non-frequency snapshot matrix $[\bw_1,\ldots,\bw_K]$ of
\eqref{eq:freqsnap}, with $\bw_k=\ba_k\otimes\bh_k$ and
$\ba_k=\beta_k\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}\bBm_k\be_k$, has rank $K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{IEEEproof}
Complete $\bU_S$ to a unitary $[\bU_S,\bU_S^{\perp}]$. The subspace
inclusion makes $(\bU_S^{\perp})^{\mathsf H}\by\sim
\mathcal{CN}(\mathbf0,\sigma^2\bI)$, independent of $\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\by$
and free of $(\bchi,\bx)$, which gives (i); for unknown $\sigma^2$ it
involves the discarded block only through the scalar $c_\perp$, so the
factorization theorem gives (ii). Because $\bA=\bU_S\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\bA$
the cross term in the orthogonal decomposition of $\by$ vanishes, which is
\eqref{eq:isometry_short} with a data-only second term. Writing
$\bA=\bU_S\bA_c$ and $\bD_\chi=\bU_S\bD_{\chi,c}$, semi-unitarity gives
$\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi=\bU_S\bPi_{\bA_c}^{\perp}\bD_{\chi,c}$,
pseudoinverses included; taking Grams leaves \eqref{eq:efim_short}
unchanged, proving (iii). For (iv), $\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}$ acts on the EVS mode
alone and commutes with both Vandermonde frequency factors, so the noiseless
pole set is unchanged and shift-invariance identifiability survives whenever
the compressed snapshot matrix has column rank $K$.
\end{IEEEproof}

C2 separates statistical sufficiency from finite-sample algorithmic
conditioning. Equation~\eqref{eq:isometry_short} is a pointwise identity,
not an approximation, and its discarded term carries no dependence on
$(\bchi,\bx)$, so no candidate changes its score relative to any other.
Phase~I, however, is an ESPRIT-based subspace procedure, not the exact ML
optimizer, so removing the $I-r$ noise-only receive directions improves its
finite-sample recovery while leaving the criterion untouched. Pole
preservation additionally requires Theorem~\ref{thm:compress}(iv), which
holds generically once $rT\ge K$ but fails under an annihilated path, a zero
gain, or systematically dependent panel signatures. The delay-subspace
separation is therefore measured per trial in
Section~\ref{sec:simulation}.

\textit{D. C3---Certified common-clock profiling:}
\begin{theorem}[C3: Certified common-clock profiling]
\label{thm:clock}
Let $\mathcal P$ and $\mathcal T$ be the compact search sets of
Section~\ref{subsec:defchain} and assume
$\bG_\lambda(\bp)=\bG_0(\bp)+\bR_{\rm dir}(\bp)\succ\mathbf0$ on $\mathcal P$, which
for $d_{\rm evs}<2$ needs at least $\lambda_k>0$; otherwise read every
$\bG_\lambda^{-1}$ below as $\bG_\lambda^{+}$, exact since $\bb\in\coll(\bG_\lambda)$.

\textbf{(i) Exact nesting.} Analytic Jones elimination and global inner
clock maximization nest the penalized criterion \eqref{eq:penalized_short}
exactly:
$F(\bp,\Delta t)=\min_{\bx}J(\bp,\Delta t,\bx)=\|\by\|^2-Q(\bp,\Delta t)$,
\begin{equation}
 \min_{\bp\in\mathcal P}\widetilde F(\bp)
 =\min_{\bp\in\mathcal P,\,\Delta t\in\mathcal T,\,\bx\in\C^{2K}}
 J(\bp,\Delta t,\bx),
 \label{eq:nestval_short}
\end{equation}
and the argument sets correspond: pairing any outer minimizer $\bp^\star$
with a global inner clock maximizer $\Delta t^\star$ of $Q(\bp^\star,\cdot)$
and with
$\bx^\star=\bG_\lambda^{-1}(\bp^\star)\bb(\bp^\star,\Delta t^\star)$ recovers
a joint minimizer.

\textbf{(ii) Unit invariance.} The outer profile is invariant under every
invertible affine clock-unit reparameterization with $\mathcal T$
transformed accordingly.

\textbf{(iii) Certified clock solution.} The interval bound
\eqref{eq:bound_short} guarantees at every visited position
\begin{equation}
 Q(\Delta t^\star)-Q(\hat{\Delta t})\le g_{\rm cert}
 =U-Q(\hat{\Delta t}),
 \label{eq:cert_short}
\end{equation}
and $g_{\rm cert}\le\epsilon_{\rm clk}$ whenever termination occurs by the
prescribed tolerance rather than the subdivision cap.
\end{theorem}

\begin{IEEEproof}
(i) $\bG_\lambda\succ\mathbf0$ makes \eqref{eq:jquad} a strictly convex
quadratic in $\bx$, so the inner minimum $\|\by\|^2-Q$ of
\eqref{eq:vp_short} is attained at every $(\bp,\Delta t)$; minimization may
then be performed in any order, which is \eqref{eq:nestval_short}, and the
correspondence of argument sets follows from the same attainment. (ii) An
invertible affine change of clock unit is a bijection of $\mathcal T$ onto
its image, so the attained value set of $Q(\bp,\cdot)$, hence
$\widetilde F$, is unchanged. (iii) The curvature bound $L_2$ derived from
\eqref{eq:trig_short} holds uniformly on $\mathcal T$, so
\eqref{eq:bound_short} is valid on every interval. The solver maintains a
finite cover of $\mathcal T$ and records the upper bound of every interval it
discards, so $U$ dominates the bound of every cell of the cover and
$Q(\Delta t^\star)\le U$.
This proves \eqref{eq:cert_short} under any stopping rule: the gap stays
valid even when the subdivision cap is reached, and only the tolerance test
depends on the cap.
\end{IEEEproof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:clock} globalizes the conditional clock solve and removes
its outer coordinate; the remaining three-dimensional position search is
local. Table~\ref{tab:c3_control} reports the clock-unit and certificate
controls.

\textit{E. Complexity:}
Phase~I is dominated by the $N\times rT$ delay-subspace step and the
factorial first tier of association. By Theorem~\ref{thm:compress}, Phase~II
evaluates the exact forward model on the compressed observation, cutting
residual and Jacobian rows from $M=I\,N\,T$ to $M_r=r\,N\,T$ without
approximating the criterion. Each outer position evaluation costs
\begin{equation}
	C_{\rm outer}=\underbrace{O(qM_r)}_{\text{residual}}
	+\underbrace{O\big(q^2(N+T)\big)}_{\text{Gram}}
	+\underbrace{O\big(q^2N^2+qM_c\log M_c+q^3\big)}_{\text{clock profile}}
	+\,C_{\rm cert},
	\label{eq:cost}
\end{equation}
with $q=2K$, $M_c$ the zero-padded clock-FFT length and $C_{\rm cert}$ the
branch-and-bound cost, which grows with the number of visited intervals.
Because
\eqref{eq:Ak} makes each Gram block a cached position-independent
Maxwell--Jones factor times one length-$N$ and one length-$T$ inner product,
the Gram update costs $O(q^2(N+T))$, not $O(q^2M_r)$. Since the clock
never re-enters that factorization, tightening $\epsilon_{\rm clk}$
raises only the interval count inside $C_{\rm cert}$, at $O(N)$ arithmetic
per interval and no further factorization; an explicit four-dimensional
search admits no such separation, since every clock trial changes the
residual. Measured runtime and
memory are in Section~\ref{sec:simulation}.
\section{Numerical Results}
\label{sec:simulation}

\subsection{Simulation Setup}
\label{subsec:res_setup}
\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{Reference configuration. The panel frame sets its local $z$ along
$\hat{\bm n}_k$, its local $x$ along $(\bm u_z\times\hat{\bm n}_k)$
normalized, and its local $y$ by right-handed completion, which reconstructs
$\bR_k$ exactly. Gains and Jones states are redrawn independently per trial.}
\label{tab:params}
\centering
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\quad}p{0.63\columnwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Parameter & Value\\
\midrule
Carrier & $f_c=60$~GHz, $\lambda_c=5.00$~mm\\
RIS panels & $K=3$, $M_R=64\times64$, $\lambda_c/2$ spacing\\
BS array & $M_A=16$ full-EVS ULA, $\lambda_c/2$ spacing\\
OFDM & $N=63$, $\Delta f=5$~MHz, $P=L=32$ smoothing\\
Training & $T=256$ configurations; block-static UE, clock, gains
and Jones states\\
UE / box [m] & $(1.25,0.55,0.75)$;
$[0.30,2.70]\!\times\![-1.40,1.50]\!\times\![0.35,1.45]$\\
BS [m] & $(-22.50,-12.50,2.00)$\\
RIS centres $\br_k$ [m] & $(4.20,-2.20,1.05)$; $(5.10,2.10,1.15)$;
$(4.80,0,1.25)$\\
RIS normals $\hat{\bm n}_k$ & $(-0.9159,0.4005,-0.0264)$;
$(-0.8523,-0.5205,-0.0528)$; $(-0.9940,0.0158,-0.1084)$\\
