\subsection{Robustness, Complexity and Deployment Limits}
\label{subsec:res_robustness}
Three sensitivities set the deployment envelope, two of them in
Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}; the model-order panel, the other mismatch and
generalization sweeps are supplementary.

Calibration is the binding one
(Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}(a)--(b)): RIS--BS angular error degrades
precision long before capture,
raising conditional RMSE from $0.416$ to $31.1$~mm at $0.25^\circ$ while
the basin rate holds at $99.58\%$, while at $1^\circ$ the catastrophic rate reaches $58.54\%$. Receiver gain, phase and sensor-position mismatch,
RIS element-phase error and first-order colored noise are all milder over
their tested ranges.

Model order is a material assumption, not a tuning
parameter. In the dedicated $0$-dB sweep, $\widehat K=2$ is structurally
destructive ($0.705$-m median, $P_{\rm cat}=65.6\%$), and $\widehat K=4$
and $5$ keep submillimeter medians while carrying catastrophic rates above
a third.

Delay coincidence bounds acquisition where information does not
(Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}(c)). Across a $5\times5\times2$ position grid the proposed median per-position
catastrophic rate is $0.62\%$ against $2.71\%$ for the
baseline-initialized 4D-JVP, and both hard
corners lie near a cascaded-delay coincidence surface, the worst reaching
$29.58\%$ at a minimum separation of $0.062/B_{\rm occ}$ -- while the
free-Jones EFIM stays full rank at every tested position, corners included.
Local information sufficiency therefore does not imply global acquisition
reliability: screening needs a delay-coincidence check and a coarse global
fallback, not a PEB map alone.

\label{subsec:res_cost}
On one host at $-10$~dB the proposed route requires $3.18$~s and $2.52$~GB
on the $96$ receive chains a full EVS costs, between the
baseline-initialized 4D-JVP ($2.40$~s) and
Algebraic CPD ($4.38$~s). A separate decomposition gives $2.83$~s in Phase~I and
$0.34$~s in Phase~II, so acquisition dominates the measured budget.
The breakdown is supplementary.

A $25\%$ cyclic prefix at $\Delta f=5$~MHz is $50$~ns, covering the
$19.1$-ns cascaded-delay spread plus clock-search interval, so one
configuration per symbol gives $T_{\rm train}\simeq64~\mu$s at $T=256$ and
the single-coherence-block assumption of Section~\ref{sec:system_model}
holds; the UE-displacement check is supplementary.
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We studied joint 3-D localization and clock synchronization of a
single-antenna user in a blocked-LoS distributed near-field RIS uplink
observed by one shared electromagnetic vector-sensor array. A calibrated
space--frequency/Maxwell--Jones factorization supports a two-phase estimator:
Phase~I acquires and associates cascaded factors inside a sufficient subspace
that calibration fixes before any parameter is estimated, and Phase~II
eliminates the Jones coefficients while profiling the clock along its exact
unitary orbit. At $-10$~dB the estimator reaches a $0.32$-mm median position
error at $0.42\%$ and $0.31\%$ position and clock catastrophic rates,
matching baseline-initialized 4D-JVP in precision at a sixfold lower tail.

The gain is therefore in acquisition reliability, and the broader lesson is
that a local information bound does not characterize it: the weight a reduced
receiver costs is panel- and polarization-dependent, so it can leave one panel
almost unobserved, and the free-Jones information matrix stays full rank even
where acquisition is least reliable. Receiver-mode selection and panel
deployment should be screened by Maxwell-subspace and cascaded-delay
separation, not by a bound map alone. The accuracies reported here are
established on a centre-frequency spatial manifold, under which a
per-subcarrier diagnostic identifies the clock, not the position, as the
sensitive quantity. More generally, neither mechanism behind Phase~II is
specific to a Maxwell--Jones receiver: calibration turns sufficiency into a
property of the infrastructure whenever a terminal block of a cascade is fixed
in advance, and any nuisance acting as a common unitary phase ramp can be
profiled globally and removed from the outer search. Together they point to a
structured route to estimation in vector-sensor arrays, of which the shared
clock treated here is one instance; within the calibrated, known-panel-count
model, a polynomial-time association surrogate for larger panel counts is the
most direct opening.

